The '''Koenigsegg CC8S''' is a [[Mid-engine design|mid-engine]] [[sports car]] produced by the Swedish automobile manufacturer [[Koenigsegg]]. It was the company's first production automobile and the first production car to use the company's trademark dihedral synchro-helix actuation doors. At its introduction, it won several awards, including the Guinness World Record for the Most Powerful Production Engine and design awards from both [[Red Dot Design Award|Red Dot]] in Germany and Utmärkt Svensk Form in Sweden.<ref name="Koenigsegg CC8S History section">{{Cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/our-cars-yesterday/all-cars/cc8s/ |title=CC8S Page at Koenigsegg website |accessdate=2011-11-01 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20111007050521/http://www.koenigsegg.com/our-cars-yesterday/all-cars/cc8s/ |archivedate=2011-10-07 }}</ref>

==Development==
The CC8S was developed from the [[Koenigsegg CC|CC]] prototype. It was the culmination of 8 years of research and development. Despite limited resources, the chassis, suspension, brakes, and several other components were designed in-house by Koenigsegg. The pre-production car was shown at the 2000 [[Paris Auto Show]], the public's reaction to the car was favourable and international contacts were made. The car had many unique functions such as the roof, which could be stored in the car's trunk located in the front similar to how a [[Chevrolet Corvette]] stores its roof (other Targa top sports cars of the time such as the [[Ferrari F50]] lacked this feature), vertical opening doors dubbed [[dihedral synchro-helix]] actuation doors and a central patented free-flowing exhaust system based on the 'Rocket Cat Principle'. These unique features and design received great praise from the spectators, despite large orders, only 6 cars were produced due to high production costs out of which, 2 were right-hand drive. The CC8S was replaced by the more powerful [[Koenigsegg CCR|CCR]] model in 2004.<ref name="CC8S">{{cite web|url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/model/cc8s|title=Koenigsegg CC8S|work=Koenigsegg.com|accessdate=2024-06-14}}</ref>

==Overview==
===Body===
[[File:Koenigsegg CC8S - Side 01 (cropped).jpg|left|thumb|Rear view]]
The body is a two-door, two-seat [[targa top]] design, meaning a portion of the roof can be removed and stored under the bonnet. The chassis is made from [[kevlar]]-reinforced [[carbon fibre]]. The kevlar-reinforced carbon fibre semi-monocoque is attached to a steel subframe at the front of the chassis, and an aluminium subframe at the rear.

The engine, transmission, and rear suspension are mounted to the aluminium subframe. All body panels are made from carbon fibre, and [[venturi effect|venturi]] tunnels and diffusers occupy the bottom of the car. These components increase downforce in conjunction with a rear-mounted spoiler. The frontal area of the car is just {{CdA|link=car|{{convert|1.825|sqm|sqft}}}}, which, in conjunction with the car's low {{Cd|long=yes|0.30}}, affords the car very low overall aerodynamic drag.

===Engine and Transmission===
The CC8S is powered by a [[supercharged]] 4.7&ndash;litre [[DOHC]] [[V8 engine]] based on the [[Ford Modular engine|Ford Modular]] design but made of cast aluminium with [[Multi-valve#Four valves|4 valves]] per cylinder and carbon fibre covers weighing {{convert|210|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}}, with a compression ratio of 8.6:1. It produces {{Convert|664|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} at 6,800 rpm and {{convert|750|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque at 5,000 rpm. The redline is set to 7,250 rpm. The engine powers the rear wheels through a 6-speed manual transmission specially developed for the car by [[Costruzione Italiana Macchine Attrezzi|CIMA]].<ref name="CC8S"/>

===Wheels===
The CC8S uses magnesium alloy wheels with center-locking hubs, to which 225/40ZR 18" front and 335/40ZR 20" rear [[Michelin]] Pilot Sport 2 tyres are mounted. The brake discs are cross-drilled for increased ventilation, and measure {{convert|340|mm|in|1|abbr=on}} at the front and {{convert|315|mm|in|1|abbr=on}} at the rear. The brake calipers contain 6 pistons in front and 4 pistons at the rear. The brakes are power-assisted by the Koenigsegg Advanced Control System.

===Performance===
'''Manufacturer estimates'''
* Acceleration: {{convert|0–100|km/h|abbr=on}} under 3.5&nbsp;seconds
* Top speed: {{convert|390|km/h|mi/h|abbr=on}}<ref name="CC8S"/>
* Standing {{convert|1/4|mi|m|0}}: 10&nbsp;seconds, trap speed {{convert|217|km/h|mi/h|abbr=on}}<ref name="CC8S"/>
* Braking distance: {{convert|32|m|0|abbr=on}} {{convert|100–0|km/h|abbr=on}}
